   176	
   177	**2** **BACKGROUND ON GITHUB ACTIONS**
   178	
   179	
   180	GitHub Actions is a platform for automating CI/CD (continuous integration/continuous deployment) tasks within the GitHub ecosystem. Developers use the platform, e.g., for automatically executing
   181	tests on every new commit or for regularly deploying applications.
   182	The central concept of the platform are _workflows_, which are executable processes that perform one or more _jobs_ when a specific
   183	_trigger event_ occurs. The jobs within a workflow are either executed
   184	
   185	
   186	
   187	in parallel, sequentially as declared in the workflow, or in an order
   188	constrained by dependencies between jobs. Each job consists of
   189	sequentially executed _steps_, each representing either a command,
   190	such as executing a bash script, or an _action_, i.e., reusable code that
   191	implements a common CI/CD-related tasks, e.g., checking out code.
   192	The triggering events that start workflows include pushing to a
   193	repository, creating a new pull requests, and also scheduled events
   194	that happen regularly, similar to a cron job.
   195	Jobs are executed on _virtual machines (VMs)_, where each job is
   196	executed inside its own VM runner. That is, two jobs cannot be
   197	executed in parallel on the same VM. We call each execution of a
   198	job a _job run_ or simply a _run_ . A workflow execution includes at
   199	least one job run. GitHub offers VMs with different versions of
   200	Linux, macOS, and Windows, and also supports self-hosted VMs
   201	on a user’s own infrastructure.
   202	
   203	All computations performed on GitHub-hosted VMs are billed
   204	on a per-minute basis. For example, as of March 2023, running a
   205	minute of computation on a standard Linux VM (2 CPUs, 7GB RAM,
   206	14GB disk space) costs $0.008. In addition to CPU time, GitHub also
   207	charges costs for disk space, [1] which we do not account for in this
   208	study. GitHub offers free resources to public repositories, e.g., 2,000
   209	free minutes of computation to each unpaid user account. To help
   210	developers manage the CI/CD costs, GitHub Actions offers several
   211	optimization mechanisms, e.g., caching, failing quickly if one out
   212	of a set of related jobs fails, or skipping workflows for particular
   213	trigger events.
   214	
   215	
   216	**3** **METHODOLOGY**
   217	
   218	
   219	To answer the research questions listed in Section 1, we gather a
   220	dataset of 1.3 million workflow runs from 952 open-source repositories on GitHub. To the best of our knowledge, this dataset is
   221	the largest of its kind. The following describes our methodology
   222	for gathering the dataset (Section 3.1), a set of metrics to quantify
   223	the resource usage of workflows (Section 3.2), and a name-based
   224	analysis to determine what CI/CD tasks a job performs (Section 3.3)
   225	followed by the methodology for estimating the prevalence and
   226	impact of optimizations (Section 3.4). Figure 1 gives an overview of
   227	our methodology.
   228	
   229	
   230	**3.1** **Data Collection**
   231	
   232	
   233	We collect a dataset of workflow runs in three steps. First, we sample
   234	600 repositories from a set of 67K repositories collected by prior
   235	work [ 9 ], which have at least one workflow, 100 stars, and 100
   236	commits. To also study less popular projects, we augment the initial
   237	set with 352 further repositories, which we obtain by randomly
   238	sampling 74K repositories with less than 100 stars from a larger
   239	set of 735K repositories [ 8 ] and by then keeping only those that
   240	are using workflows. The combined list results in a dataset of 952
   241	repositories. Second, we use the GitHub API to obtain all workflow
   242	runs for each selected repository. The gathered information includes
   243	the date and time of each workflow run, the workflow ID, the branch
   244	or commit that triggered the workflow, and other metadata. Third,
   245	we use the GitHub API to gather details about the jobs that were
   246	executed within each workflow run. These data include, e.g., the
