     1	# Resource Usage and Optimization Opportunities in Workflows of GitHub Actions
     2	
     3	
     4	
     5	[![DOI](https://zenodo.org/badge/DOI/10.5281/zenodo.8344575.svg)](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.8344575) [<img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/dockerhub-GH_workflows_study-blue.svg?logo=Docker">](https://hub.docker.com/r/islemdockerdev/github-workflow-resource-study)
     6	
     7	This artifact submission includes the code and data utilized in our empirical study described in the [ICSE'24 paper "Resource Usage and Optimization Opportunities in Workflows of GitHub Actions"](https://software-lab.org/publications/icse2024_workflows.pdf). The study produces two primary outputs:
     8	
     9	1. **Dataset:** We collected a dataset comprising 1.3 million runs with 3.7 million jobs.
    10	
    11	2. **Analysis Code and Results:** The code and results of our analysis conducted on the collected dataset.
    12	
    13	Both components can be accessed and explored using the instructions provided below.
    14	
    15	## Badges to claim
    16	To acknowledge our artifact, we seek to claim two badges:
    17	1. **Available badge**
    18	2. **Reusable badge**
    19	
    20	## Requirements
    21	The following sections outline the requirements for importing, installing, and using our artifact.
    22	### User Requirements
    23	1. **Familiarity with Python Virtual Environment**: Ability to create a Python virtual environment and install our tool within it. Step-by-step instructions are provided in this README file, but prior knowledge of this task is advantageous.
    24	
    25	2. **Working with Jupyter Notebooks**: Capability to install and launch JupyterLab notebooks. Detailed instructions will be provided.
    26	
    27	3. **Working with VScode**: Capability to install and launch VScode. Detailed instructions will be provided.
    28	
    29	### Hardware Requirements
    30	1. **Disk Space**: A minimum of 8GB disk space is required for our collected data, external data files, and our portable Python environment.
    31	        
    32	2. **RAM free space**: Some data preprocessing requires substantial RAM space. In our experiments, usage can extend up to 9GB of RAM for a single analysis notebook (on top of the overhead of other software such as VScode, Docker or JupyerLab when needed).
    33	
    34	3. **CPU cores**: None of our scripts use multiprocessing. However, for reference, we conducted our experiments on a machine with 48 CPU cores. The time taken by our machine is specified within each code notebook.
    35	
    36	### Software Requirements
    37	1. **Operating System and Docker**: Our scripts are designed to run on a Linux machine (Ubuntu 20.04). For optimal compatibility, it is recommended to rerun them on an Ubuntu machine with a version close to 20.04. We also recommend the usage of Docker v20.x or higher.
    38	
    39	2. **Python Version**: The implementation and used packages are compatible with Python 3.8 (recommended) and above.
    40	
    41	3. **Jupyter-Lab or VScode**: Since we use notebooks to present our analysis, you will need a notebook runner. It is preferable to have JupyterLab or VSCode installed for running notebooks.
    42	
    43	4. **GitHub Username and Token**: To execute the collection process using the GitHub API, you need to add at least one token to the file tokens.txt (see data collection part below).
    44	
    45	## How to setup the artifact?
    46	You can setup the artifact following one of the three options below:
    47	
    48	### Option 1: Docker image from DockerHub:
    49	The perhaps most straightforward way to utilize our artifact is by pulling our Docker image from DockerHub. Follow these steps:
    50	1. Execute the following commands in your terminal to retrieve and start our docker image
    51	    ```bash
    52	    # Pull image
    53	    docker pull islemdockerdev/github-workflow-resource-study:v1.1
    54	    # Run the image inside a container
    55	    docker run -itd --name github-study islemdockerdev/github-workflow-resource-study:v1.1
    56	    # Start the container
    57	    docker start -i github-study
    58	    ```
    59	
    60	2. After starting the container, open VSCode and navigate to the containers icon on the left panel (Ensure that you have the Remote Explorer extension installed on your VScode).
    61	
    62	3. Under the Dev Containers tab, locate the name of the container you just started (e.g., github-study).
    63	
    64	4. Finally, attach the container **github-study** to a new window by clicking the '+' sign located on the right of the container's name. Navigate to **workdir** folder in vscode window (all the files of the artifact are located there).
    65	
    66	**Tutorial reference:** For detailed steps on how to attach a docker container in VScode, please refer to this short video tutorial (1min 38 sec): https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gUtN5j4QnY&t
    67	
    68	### Option 2: Get our Zenodo image
    69	1. Download our shared Zip from: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.8344575
    70	
    71	2. After unzipping the file, load our Docker image using the command:
    72	    ```bash
    73	    # load the container
    74	    docker image load -i ./github_study_container.tar
    75	    # run and attach (careful with the name)
    76	    docker run -it islemdockerdev/github-workflow-resource-study:v1.1
    77	    ```
    78	3. Attach the docker container to a vscode window as demonstrated in here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gUtN5j4QnY&t
    79	4. Navigate to the folder **workdir** in vscode window (all the files of the artifact are located there).
    80	
    81	### Option 3: Manually prepare the environement
    82	
    83	If you prefer a manual setup, follow these steps. 
    84	
    85	1. Ensure you have Python 3.8 or higher installed. In your terminal, in the same folder as this README file, execute the following commands:
    86	
    87	```bash
    88	# create a python environement, preferably use Python 3.8 or higher
    89	python3.8 -m venv .venv
    90	# activate the environement
    91	source .venv/bin/activate
    92	# install requirements
    93	pip install -r requirements.txt
    94	# install the current folder as a Python package
    95	pip install .
    96	# create necessary folders
    97	mkdir tables
    98	mkdir repositories_runs
    99	# install jupyterlab
   100	pip install jupyterlab
   101	# launch jupyterlab
   102	jupyter-lab
   103	```
   104	
   105	2. Following that, download our dataset release from: https://github.com/sola-st/github-workflow-resource-optimization/releases/tag/v1.0.0
   106	3. Unzip the dataset in the same folder as this README file.
   107	
   108	## Running the Artifact
   109	
   110	### Data Collection
   111	In this part, we present the necessary resources and instructions to replicate our data collection process (or collect more data).
   112	
   113	#### Collection resources
   114	* [repositories.csv](./repositories.csv): This is the list of popular repositories ([reference to this work](https://decan.lexpage.net/files/ICSME-2022.pdf)) from which we randomly sampled 600 repositories to use them in our study.
   115	
   116	* [repositories-2021-03-08.zip](./repositories-2021-03-08.csv): The list containing unpopular repositories ([reference to this work](https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.04682)) from which we randomly sampled 74K candidates to collect workflows data. We ended up with 352 repositories having such data.
   117	
   118	* [Collection notebook](./collect.ipynb): This is the code (in notebook format) used to collect our data using the two repositories lists as input.
   119	
   120	
   121	#### How to run?
   122	To run the collection code:
   123	1. Open the notebook [Collection notebook](./collect.ipynb).
   124	2. Open the file [tokens.json](./tokens.json) and add your GitHub tokens into the file. To have a non-interrupted collection, we used 4 tokens at a time granting 20K requests per hour in total.
   125	Here is an example of how the tokens.json file should look like:
   126	    ```json
   127	    [
   128	        "here you put token 1",
   129	        "here you put token 2",
   130	    ]
   131	    ```
   132	3. Run the cells of the notebook one after another.
   133	
   134	#### Resulting Data
   135	Applying the above collection process we obtained the following data files (In case you are viewing this on GitHub, the links below wouldn't work but you can find all the files within [this relase](https://github.com/sola-st/github-workflow-resource-optimization/releases/tag/v1.0.0)):
   136	* [all_runs.csv](./all_runs.csv)
   137	* [all_job.csv](./all_job.csv)
   138	* [all_steps.csv](./all_steps.csv)
   139	* [all_commits.csv](./all_commits.csv)
   140	* [all_actors.csv](./all_actors.csv)
   141	* [all_repositories.csv](./all_repositories.csv)
   142	* [all_pull_steps.csv](./all_pull_steps.csv)
   143	
